<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Quadratic Forms and Their Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01904</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Quadratic Forms and Their Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>06aaf20b-598f-4c01-b8b3-2dfd657d813b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">In an earlier lesson, we saw that an equation such as <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>h</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>t</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>10</m:mn>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>78</m:mn>
            <m:mi>t</m:mi>
            <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
            <m:mn>16</m:mn>
            <m:msup>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:msup>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> can model the
        height of an object thrown upward from a height of 10 feet with a vertical velocity of 78
        feet per second.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
        <media alt="A young man in a purple sweater stands on railroad tracks in a forest, looking up as he juggles or catches a red apple above his hands. Lush green trees frame the scene.">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_03_01.jpg" width="300"/>
        </media>

      </para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">1. </span>Is the expression <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>16</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>78</m:mn>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>10</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> written in standard
            form? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00005">Yes. It is a sum of a squared term, a linear term, and a constant
            term. Written in the form <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>c</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, – 16 is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, 78 is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, and 10 is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>c</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">2. </span>Paola said that the equation <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> also
            defines the same function, written in factored form. Is Paola correct? Be prepared to
            show your reasoning.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00008">Yes. Distribute the – 2 to <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and you
            have <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>16</m:mn>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.
            Multiply this binomial by <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. The
            result of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>16</m:mn>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>16</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>78</m:mn>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>10</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
        <para id="para-00019" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">3. </span>Here is a graph representing both <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>g</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mn>8</m:mn>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>h</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>10</m:mn>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>78</m:mn>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
              <m:mn>16</m:mn>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>. <media alt="A parabola on a coordinate grid. The x-axis scale is 1 and extends from 0 to 6. The y-axis scale is 10 extends from 0 to 140.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_03_02.svg"/>
          </media></para>
      

      <exercise id="_930228bf-fe65-44d8-aee1-7dc87ea500a2" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">

          <para id="para-00009">a. What is the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-value of the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-intercept?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">The <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
         
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="adf70d37-f2a9-40c1-b144-00dcb99942b0" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">

          <para id="para-00012">b. What is the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-value of the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-intercept?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00013">The <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
    
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00015">c. What do each of these points mean in this situation?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00017">The <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-intercept shows the
            height, in feet, of the object when it was thrown, and the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-intercept shows the
            number of seconds that passed after the object was thrown before it hit the ground.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>

    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00018"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_7_10_2_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
    </note>

    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Characteristics of Quadratic Graphs</title>
        <para id="para-00021">The graph of <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> is shown
          below.</para>
        <para id="para-00022">What are the <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>- and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>-intercepts?</para>
        <para id="para-00023">
          <media alt="A parabola on a coordinate grid. The x-axis scale is 5 and extends from approximately negative 10 to 10. The y-axis scale is 5 and extends from approximately negative 10 to 12.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_05_01.jpg" width="300"/>
          </media>

        </para>
        <para id="para-00024">The <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>-intercepts are where the graph
          crosses the <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>-axis.</para>
        <para id="para-00025">This happens at <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo>,</m:mo>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo>,</m:mo>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00026">The <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>-intercept is where the graph
          crosses the <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>-axis.</para>
        <para id="para-00027">This happens at <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              <m:mo>,</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Characteristics of Quadratic Graphs</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00004">
            <problem id="prob-00006">
              <para id="para-00028">The graph of <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          is below.</para>
              <para id="para-00029">What are the <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>- and <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-intercepts of
          the graph?</para>
              <para id="para-00030">
                <media alt="A parabola on a coordinate grid. The x-axis scale is 5 and extends from approximately negative 10 to 10. The y-axis scale is 5 and extends from approximately negative 10 to 12.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_05_02.jpg" width="300"/>
                </media>

              </para>
              <para id="para-00031"/>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00006">
              <para id="para-00032">Compare your answer: </para>
              <para id="para-00033">Here is how to find the intercepts:</para>
              <para id="para-00034">For the <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-intercepts:
          Look at where the graph crosses the <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-axis.</para>
              <para id="para-00035">This happens at <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>. So the
          points are <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          and <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          .</para>
              <para id="para-00036">For the <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-intercept:
          Look at where the graph crosses the <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-axis.</para>
              <para id="para-00037">This happens at <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> so the point
          is <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          .</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>